tion/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=2,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-5)+0+2=-3.$" "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=2,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-5)+0+2=-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393646 :question/problem "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].$" "If $\\,u(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393646 :question/worked-solution "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=8u(t)+7v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(8{e}^{-t})^{′} \\\\ (7{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=8u(t)+7v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(8{e}^{-t})^{′} \\\\ (7{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393648 :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393649 :answer/value "Aw=[\\begi