\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -16 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393877 :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=-2 \\\\ x+3y+9z=0 \\\\ y+6z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=-2 \\\\ x+3y+9z=0 \\\\ y+6z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393877 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=-3,z=1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+(-3)+1=-2.$" "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=-3,z=1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+(-3)+1=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393884 :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-3y+2z=-4 \\\\ -4x+4y-3z=7 \\\\ x-y+z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-3y+2z=-4 \\\\ -4x+4y-3z=7 \\\\ x-y+z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393884 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=1,y=2,z=-1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=1+2+(-1)=2.$" "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=1,y=2,z=-1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=1+2+(-1)=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393912 :question/problem "If $Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, then ${Z}^{5}=$" "If $Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${Z}^{5}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186393912 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${Z}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{2} & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${Z}^{2}=Z,$ so $Z$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${Z}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{5} & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Z}^{5}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, we compute ${Z}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{2} & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${Z}^{2}=Z,$ so $Z$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${Z}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{5} & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot Z \\\\  & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & ={Z}^{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Z}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393914 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393915 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393916 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393917 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393918 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393961 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393961 :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ {c}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4\\cdot 3+{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+(-8)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ {c}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4\\cdot 3+{c}_{2}=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+(-8)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393963 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186393964 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186393965 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186393966 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186393967 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}"] [:db/cas 17592186393996 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$" "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393996 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =10.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393998 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186393999 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394000 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394001 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394002 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394037 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y+3z \\\\ 2x+y+z \\\\ x-y+5z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x+y+3z \\\\ 2x+y+z \\\\ x-y+5z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186394037 :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (-2)+3+3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+3+k \\\\ -2-3+5\\cdot k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3+3k \\\\ -1+k \\\\ -5+5k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3+3k=0 \\\\ -1+k=0 \\\\ -5+5k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=1.$" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot (-2)+3+3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+3+k \\\\ -2-3+5\\cdot k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3+3k \\\\ -1+k \\\\ -5+5k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3+3k=0 \\\\ -1+k=0 \\\\ -5+5k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186394052 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394053 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394054 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394055 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394056 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394078 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186394078 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+5([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+5(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394080 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394081 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394082 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394083 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394084 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394085 :question/problem "![](images/36/369dde2e95b52356b71972b8f1103430e0a840d3d0478626dc5a78cba0f51a41.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $ABCD$ is shown above. Which of the following is ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′},$ the image of $ABCD$ under the action of $T?$" "![](images/36/369dde2e95b52356b71972b8f1103430e0a840d3d0478626dc5a78cba0f51a41.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $ABCD$ is shown above. Which of the following is ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′},$ the image of $ABCD$ under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186394085 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 8 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}$ are $(-1,3),(0,6),(0,8),(-1,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png)" "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}$ are $(-1,3),(0,6),(0,8),(-1,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3caf43c4c62614f0c38b2f32c12892820ab4ae732669767b0d50104c85eee605.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186394092 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =7.$*" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =7.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186394092 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}-3[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394094 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394095 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394096 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394097 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394098 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394100 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394101 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 0 \\\\ 0 & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 0 \\\\ 0 & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394102 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394103 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394104 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394105 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy})={e}^{xy}+xy\\,\\text{ }{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})={e}^{xy}+xy\\,\\text{ }{e}^{xy}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{xy})={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy})={e}^{xy}+xy\\,\\text{ }{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})={e}^{xy}+xy\\,\\text{ }{e}^{xy}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{xy})={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394107 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy} \\\\ {e}^{xy} & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy} \\\\ {e}^{xy} & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394108 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394109 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}+xy \\\\ {e}^{xy}+xy & {e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}+xy \\\\ {e}^{xy}+xy & {e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394110 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(2+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(2+xy) & {y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(2+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(2+xy) & {y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394111 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394112 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4y+1 & 3{y}^{2}-4x-1\\end{aligned}]$" "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y+1 & 3{y}^{2}-4x-1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186394112 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4y+1 & 3{y}^{2}-4x-1\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-4y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-4x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4y+1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-4x-1)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y+1)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-4x-1)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y+1 & 3{y}^{2}-4x-1\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-4y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-4x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4y+1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-4x-1)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y+1)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-4x-1)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394114 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6y\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394115 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4y\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4x \\\\ 4x & -4y\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394116 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2y & 4x \\\\ 4x & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2y & 4x \\\\ 4x & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394117 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6y & 4x \\\\ 4x & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6y & 4x \\\\ 4x & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394118 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394119 :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186394119 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+2\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186394133 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394133 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}b \\\\  & =4b \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}b \\\\  & =4b \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394135 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394136 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394137 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394138 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394139 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394140 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394140 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=6{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6+5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1}{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{41}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=6{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6+5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1}{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{41}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394142 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394143 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394144 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394145 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394146 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394147 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bfcc5c729475256b70211bcf18e94b605e4486790b13794eaa46cce4f6461bb.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown above. Which of the following is $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′},$ the image of $△ABC$ under the action of $T?$" "![](images/7b/7bfcc5c729475256b70211bcf18e94b605e4486790b13794eaa46cce4f6461bb.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown above. Which of the following is $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′},$ the image of $△ABC$ under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186394147 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $△ABC:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}$ are $(-3,-1),(-4,0),(-2,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/54/548c907226db9cbdc8fb4dd18c74f3a2001ba7d90973c6f433f67b3e6b87fd29.png)" "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $△ABC:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}$ are $(-3,-1),(-4,0),(-2,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/54/548c907226db9cbdc8fb4dd18c74f3a2001ba7d90973c6f433f67b3e6b87fd29.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186394161 :question/problem "![](images/61/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$" "![](images/61/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet $T=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186394161 :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](images/72/72c88d1df90c3462d7f1f186c06b9a7acf30c071f5ba8bbf722ee125fee0e616.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the rectangle shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](images/72/72c88d1df90c3462d7f1f186c06b9a7acf30c071f5ba8bbf722ee125fee0e616.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the rectangle shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394168 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394168 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to its row echelon form. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to its row echelon form. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394170 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394171 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394172 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394173 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394174 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394181 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal."] [:db/cas 17592186394181 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{24}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{35}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot 11+6\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot (-8)}{{11}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{210}{210}[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{24}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{35}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot 11+6\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot (-8)}{{11}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{210}{210}\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix