\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 10}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=5{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394444 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394445 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394446 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394447 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394448 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394449 :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186394449 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 19 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 22 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & 