}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394847 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -7 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -7 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?"] [:db/cas 17592186394847 :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{13}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -7 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}.$" "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of ${a}_{13}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -7 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -11 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}-{C}_{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186394875 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394876 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394877 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394878 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 17\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 17\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394879 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394880 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$"] [:db/cas 17592186394880 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $-4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $-4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394882 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186394883 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\