n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186394983 :question/worked-solution "Any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =2\\cdot (2t+{t}^{3})+2\\cdot (3-t-3{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4t+2{t}^{3}+6-2t-6{t}^{3} \\\\  & =6+2t-4{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[p(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =2\\cdot (2t+{t}^{3})+2\\cdot (3-t-3{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4t+2{t}^{3}+6-2t-6{t}^{3} \\\\  & =6+2t-4{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394990 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B={{B}_{1},{B}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B={{B}_{1},{B}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186394990 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 9{x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 1 & =4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix