& 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 26\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395031 :question/problem "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}?$" "$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186395031 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={18}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}17 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 17 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}=(-4)+4=0.$" "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={18}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 17 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}=(-4)+4=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395038 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395038 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395040 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395041 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395042 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395043 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395044 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395045 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395045 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations which is already in row echelon form and has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=1.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations which is already in row echelon form and has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=1.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395047 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395048 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395049 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395050 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395051 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395052 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395052 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395054 :answer/valu