$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395066 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395068 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395069 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db