 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{3})=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395276 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 & 5 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 & 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395278 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395279 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395280 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395281 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395282 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395295 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395295 :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can find the coor