at $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395665 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=7\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=7\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395667 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395668 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395669 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395670 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395671 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395769 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=1 \\\\  & y=4 \\\\  & z=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. Substituting both $y=4$ and $z=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(4)+2(-4)=1\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5,y=4$, and $z=-4.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨5,4,-4⟩.$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=1 \\\\  & y=4 \\\\  & z=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. Substituting both $y=4$ and $z=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(4)+2(-4)=1\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5,y=4$, and $z=-4.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨5,4,-4⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395818 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186395818 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{5}{2},{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{5}{2}.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{5}{2},{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395825 :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Given the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186395825 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=0,$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186395827 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395828 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395829 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395830 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395831 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395838 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" "Find an eigenvector of\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395838 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395840 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395841 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395842 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395843 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395844 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395860 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395861 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395862 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395863 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395864 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395871 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395871 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Note that all $\\lambda $'s are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Note that all $\\lambda $'s are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395873 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395874 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395875 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395876 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395885 :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{4}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}-7{x}_{4}-3{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}+5{x}_{3}+2{x}_{1} \\\\ -7{x}_{3}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{4}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}-7{x}_{4}-3{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}+5{x}_{3}+2{x}_{1} \\\\ -7{x}_{3}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395885 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -7 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5 & 1 & -9 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & -3 & -15 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14 & 0 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the foruth equation, we get the false statement $0=14.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5 & 1 & -9 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -7 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & -3 & -15 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 & 1 & 13\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14 & 0 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the foruth equation, we get the false statement $0=14.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186395898 :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if  $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if  $H=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395898 :question/worked-solution "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=0$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" "A vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=0$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395900 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395901 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395902 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395903 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395904 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186395938 :question/problem "Calculate ${Q}^{-1}$, where\n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate ${Q}^{-1}$, where\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395938 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $Q$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Q)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=(-3)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 1=1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Q)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{Q}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {Q}^{-1}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $Q$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Q)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=(-3)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 1=1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(Q)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{Q}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {Q}^{-1}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395940 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395941 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395942 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395943 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395944 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186395978 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186395978 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z=2 \\\\ 0x+y-3z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-3,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution." "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z=2 \\\\ 0x+y-3z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-3,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution."] [:db/cas 17592186395980 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2-y-z \\\\ 4-z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}2-y-z \\\\ 4-z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186395981 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ y \\\\ 4y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}3y \\\\ y \\\\ 4y-1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186395983 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-3y \\\\ y \\\\ 2+y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-3y \\\\ y \\\\ 2+y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186395984 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2-y-z \\\\ 1+z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}2-y-z \\\\ 1+z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186395991 :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186395991 :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}=kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\  & T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+T\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186395998 :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]?$" "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186395998 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=12.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $12.$" "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=12.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186396025 :question/problem "Find $AB$, if \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find $AB$, if \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186396025 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is $3\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in matrix $A$ and $1$ row in matrix $B$, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{3\\times 2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 1 & 0\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 1 & (-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot 1 & (-3)\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, $A$ is $3\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in matrix $A$ and $1$ row in matrix $B$, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{3\\times 2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 1 & 0\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 1 & (-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot 1 & (-3)\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 